Subject: [PATCH] ios: smaller bounds on the content caches

On an iOS host the app runs under a 3 GB limit (the increased-memory-limit
entitlement is not kept by a free sideload), and a macOS guest needs 2 GB of it
for its own RAM. The three guest-content memos at 128 MB each, the GVA encode
cache at 128 MB and the Metal resident targets at 96 MB could together take
600 MB of the ~1 GB left; measured on the phone, Metal (IOAccelerator) and
large mallocs grew by ~400 MB in the minute after the login window came up,
and the system killed the app at the limit.

On iOS only: memos 24 MB each, GVA cache 32 MB, residents 32 MB. Every one of
these is a cache whose eviction is lawful — the content is in the guest's own
pages — so a smaller bound costs re-decodes and uploads, never content.

diff --git a/crates/reims-vgpu/src/backend/metal/resident.rs b/crates/reims-vgpu/src/backend/metal/resident.rs
index bce41abf..d08937aa 100644
--- a/crates/reims-vgpu/src/backend/metal/resident.rs
+++ b/crates/reims-vgpu/src/backend/metal/resident.rs
@@ -71,7 +71,12 @@ const MAX_RESIDENTS: usize = 24;
 /// 96 MB is twelve 1920x1080 BGRA8 attachments. Sized against what a driven
 /// macos-13 boot actually asks for rather than against the host's memory:
 /// `metal_resident_evicted` is the counter that says whether it is too small.
+#[cfg(not(target_os = "ios"))]
 const MAX_RESIDENT_BYTES: u64 = 96 << 20;
+/// On iOS the app's whole budget beside the guest's RAM is about a gigabyte,
+/// measured on the phone as what ran out. 32 MB is sixteen 960x540 targets.
+#[cfg(target_os = "ios")]
+const MAX_RESIDENT_BYTES: u64 = 32 << 20;
 
 /// What identifies one retained colour target.
 ///
diff --git a/crates/reims-vgpu/src/model/state.rs b/crates/reims-vgpu/src/model/state.rs
index ffb1f9ff..4b86f8bf 100644
--- a/crates/reims-vgpu/src/model/state.rs
+++ b/crates/reims-vgpu/src/model/state.rs
@@ -2661,7 +2661,13 @@ pub struct PendingWork {
 /// `ref_texture_view_memo`, `mapper_ref_texture_memo`). A cap crossing evicts the coldest entries
 /// down to a low-water mark — never a bulk clear — so the hot working set (and
 /// its avoided re-decode/re-convert cost) survives.
+#[cfg(not(target_os = "ios"))]
 pub const GUEST_LINEAR_MEMO_BYTE_CAP: usize = 128 << 20;
+/// A phone's app runs under a 3 GB limit that the guest's own RAM takes two of,
+/// and three memos at 128 MB each were most of the rest. 24 MB still holds a
+/// dozen 960x540 frames; eviction only costs a re-decode.
+#[cfg(target_os = "ios")]
+pub const GUEST_LINEAR_MEMO_BYTE_CAP: usize = 24 << 20;
 
 /// Byte cap for the GVA-keyed normal-texture encode cache
 /// ([`DeviceState::host_gva_surfaces`]). Same basis and same value as
@@ -2708,7 +2714,11 @@ pub const GUEST_LINEAR_MEMO_BYTE_CAP: usize = 128 << 20;
 /// this exact case — an entry read every frame but never rewritten (a wallpaper
 /// plane) is touched on every hit, so it is the *hottest* thing in the map and
 /// can never be the victim. Eviction reaches only entries nothing has looked at.
+#[cfg(not(target_os = "ios"))]
 pub const GVA_ENCODE_CACHE_BYTE_CAP: usize = 128 << 20;
+/// On iOS, for the reason [`GUEST_LINEAR_MEMO_BYTE_CAP`] gives there.
+#[cfg(target_os = "ios")]
+pub const GVA_ENCODE_CACHE_BYTE_CAP: usize = 32 << 20;
 
 /// How many evicted keys [`GvaEvictionWitness`] remembers.
 ///
